# Users and roles

Register your [Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview) configuration, and the
admin shows its users and groups. Register
[authorization](/docs/auth/roles) as well, and it shows roles:

```gleam
import howdy/admin
import howdy/authorization as access

let assert Ok(permissions) = access.new(db)

admin.new()
|> admin.auth(identity)
|> admin.authorization(permissions)
```

`admin.auth` also registers auth's Repo with the
[database browser](/docs/admin/database), unless `admin.database` was given
another. `admin.authorization` needs `admin.auth`, since roles are held by
users.

## Users

The users page lists every account. From there you can:

- create a user. It is provisioned without a credential, as with
  [`auth.provision`](/docs/auth/administration), so they sign in with an
  emailed token.
- suspend a user, and resume them
- see their live [sessions](/docs/auth/sessions), with the sign-in method,
  when it started, when it was last seen, when it expires and the client,
  and revoke any one of them or all at once
- move them to another [group](/docs/auth/groups)
- see and change the roles they hold, when authorization is registered
- delete the account

Deleting asks you to type the user's email address back. It is only
offered when your app has configured `auth.with_account_deletion`, whose
function removes your app's own rows in the same transaction; see
[Account changes](/docs/auth/account).

## Groups

The groups page lists your groups. Create them, rename them, delete them,
and see who is in each.

## Roles

With authorization registered, the roles page lists every role in every
scope, with its permissions and who holds it. You can:

- define a role, global or in an organization, with its permissions one
  per line
- replace a role's permissions later
- assign it to a user and revoke it, from the role's page or from the
  user's
- delete it, which drops its assignments

## Signing in as a user

Each user's page has **Sign in as this user**, for seeing the app as they
do. It:

1. issues a session for them with `auth.impersonate`, with the method
   `Impersonation`
2. records `session.impersonated` in the audit trail, with the actor
   `howdy_admin`
3. sets the app's own session cookie, and sends you to `/`

The admin shares the app's origin, so the cookie is the real one: your next
request to the app is signed in as that user. Sign out as usual to end it.

Suspended users cannot be signed in as. The session skips second factors
and single sign-on, which is why an impersonation session is never
something a user can start themselves.

The `Impersonation` method needs auth's migration 18. Run auth's
[migrations](/docs/auth/migrations) before using this version.
